<!--
  Bimodal outcomes: two End Conditions whose runs take clearly different
  lengths, so a batch is a mixture of two populations rather than one.

  This is the case per-outcome statistics exist for. Pool the two and Tukey's
  fences come from the quartiles of the majority alone: about 80% of runs are
  Fast, so both pooled quartiles fall among Fast runs and every Slow run lands
  beyond the upper fence. Pooled detection then reports the whole Slow outcome
  as "unusual", though each Slow run is ordinary for a Slow run. Judged within
  its own outcome, it is not.

  How it works. At start, Coin drops one token into Tray; the dice gate sends
  it to Fast Mode (80%) or Slow Mode (20%). Each mode pool activates its own
  chain through a ">= 1" state connection, so exactly one chain runs per run.
  A chain's coin feeds a dice gate with two competing 50% branches, Hits and
  Misses; two branches are what make the routing random, as in
  skewed-run-length.xml. Fast Finish fires at 5 hits, Slow Finish at 30.

  Why this spread. Needing k hits at even odds gives a negative binomial run
  length, which is wide rather than bunched: over 200-run batches (seeds
  0 to 199) Fast runs take about 12 steps (sd 3, IQR 4, 7 to 23, 17 distinct
  lengths) and Slow runs about 61 (sd 8, IQR 13). That width is deliberate: no
  single run length is common enough at a Tukey fence to trip the report's
  coarse-data safeguard (0 candidates dropped across those seeds), so this
  fixture shows per-outcome detection itself. Compare probabilistic-race.xml,
  whose runs only ever take 6 to 10 steps.

  Why 80/20 and 200 runs. The minority outcome is where pooling fails, and it
  still needs 20 completed runs to be judged at all: at 200 runs it gets 32 to
  42 across those seeds. Every run finishes far inside the default 1000-tick
  cap, so nothing is censored.
-->
<diagram>
